What:		/sys/class/amhdmitx/amhdmitx0/native_hdr_mute_bypass
Description:
		Experimental native HDR10 mute-bypass policy. Read/write decimal
		values 0 or 1; other values return EINVAL. Default 0 (disabled).
		Setting 0 disables future bypass admissions; setting 1 permits
		only qualified native HDR10 events. A previously admitted zero-
		mute notification completes without compensating mute operations.
		The value reports policy, not whether a particular event qualifies.

		This control is independent of hdr_mute_frame, whose accepted
		values, legacy assertion/release behavior and global reads remain
		unchanged. Writing hdr_mute_frame does not change this policy.
		The bypass path calls neither the video nor audio mute function,
		for either assertion or release. The existing SDR zero-DRM packet
		and 1.5-second cleanup remain unchanged.

		Eligibility requires an idle HDR work lifetime with no queued,
		pre-entry or executing legacy dispatch, coalesced legacy demand or
		owed HDR assertion; a previous SDR/BT709 wire state; and a known
		unchanged successfully applied HDMI transport. This includes VIC,
		timing, fractional policy, PHY selection, color format/depth/range,
		bounded attributes, link generation and cached HDR capability.
		Unknown/invalid transport, hotplug and modesets fail qualification.

		Only the built-in native HDMI provider can receive the synchronous
		per-call plain-HDR10 tag. The G12B single-VD1, follow-source, auto-
		HDR path must have actually invoked the unforced VPP path, with
		matching native source, applied bypass and PQ output state. Dolby
		Vision, HLG, HDR10+, CUVA, conversion/forced output, skipped VPP,
		other providers and untagged/ambiguous producers use legacy policy.
		DRM and unsupported build configurations retain legacy callbacks.
		Metadata payload and public vout callback layout are unchanged.
		This is source-state qualification, not proof of displayed pixels.

		Unsupported raw/debug controls, active receiver-sense probing and
		dynamic/unknown transport controls conservatively make bypass
		ineligible until fresh driver initialization. Tracking invariant
		failures are logged and also disable eligibility. Toggling this
		control does not erase those conditions or release existing mutes.

		The legacy rapid-reentry limitation is deliberately unchanged:
		after a zero notification completes, SDR cleanup can queue; PQ
		reentry before cleanup runs can skip a fresh assertion because
		current and last mode still match, then legacy work can unmute.
		This experiment is not a general audio-ownership fix or a promise
		that every busy fallback asserts hdr_mute_frame frames.

		Off-device regression:
		  python3 tools/test-native-hdr-mute-bypass.py --negative-controls
		The test compiles actual callback/worker/producer functions under
		ASan/UBSan. Its scheduling-cutpoint model checks the kernel's
		PENDING-clear-before-callback order, not real SMP execution.
		Hardware qualification, correct HDR pixels and sink lock continuity
		require separate approved device/display validation.
